1. When training decision tree, Gaussian Naive Biases (GNB), and K nearest neigbors (Knn), I found that knn had the highest F-Score by a slim margin, with Decision Tree being a
close second and GNB performing the worst by far, likely because many features of phishing URL's are highly correlated. However, when looking at the Standard Deviation between each method, 
the decision tree comfortably had the lowest deviation, making it the most consistent, although all 3 methods performed well in this regard.

2. When looking at the confusion matricies of each method, preventing false negatives is naturally the most important metric. GNB only identified one false negative, but when comparing
with its incredibly low F-Score, the model is clearly underfit and just classifying nearly everything as a positive. 

3. I decided to perform feature importance on the decision tree model to get a better understanding of which features were most important to correctly classifying phishing URLs.
After doing some reading of feature importance, I learned about permutation importance and decided to implement that as well, leading to some interesting findings. I found that 
both importance methods returned a different feature with the highest importance score, with permutation deeming the URL of the anchor as the most important and feature importance determining
ssl final state as the most important. This makes sense as my tree likely uses the ssl final state data which is noisy to make decisions whule the URL is a more accurate classifier.

4. When choosing which model to move forward with, GNB was naturally out of the question, leaving the choice between KNN and Decision Tree. While KNN scored slightly better on F1, I opted 
for the Decision Tree model because of its considerably lower standard deviation and relative F1 score, meaning although it was performing slightly worse, it was the most consistent.

5. I implemented a MLP to see if it could get me better results that my previous algorithms and found it to be considerably better. I then did some hyperparamter tuning on the MLP and found
that naturally a higher node count per layer was better, but I need to work to find a balance that is accurate but not too computationally expensive. I did however find that the ReLU activation
function that I originally used was worse than tanh, an activation function I ha never worked with previously. After some research, I found that this was likely because while RELU just killed
any negative values in my scaled data, tanh preserved it making it a more practical choice.
